Apple external-display host checks
=================================

Run with Python 3 and a C compiler with address/undefined sanitizers:

  python3 tools/testing/selftests/drm/apple/test-afk-replies.py
  python3 tools/testing/selftests/drm/apple/test-iboot-wire.py
  python3 tools/testing/selftests/drm/apple/test-dcpext-present.py
  python3 tools/testing/selftests/drm/apple/test-dcpext-copy.py

These compile the driver's actual functions with host transport/DMA stubs.
They exercise received-length validation and late-ACK cleanup, packet bounds,
presentation ordering, failure latching and buffer ownership. They do not
establish firmware behavior or visual correctness; those need a hardware boot.

The candidate path is opt-in with appledrm.dcpext_pageflip=1 and limited to the
14.7 firmware UUID checked in dcpext_scanout_register(). It retains two mapped
buffers, copies a complete opaque frame into the idle one, then sends
SwapBegin/SetLayer/SwapEnd/SwapWait. The exact Begin ID is passed to Wait;
successful Wait is required before the old front buffer is reused. The host
wait is bounded by AFK. Failure retains both buffers and stops presentation.
Link loss is terminal in this candidate mode, including hotplug bounces after
startup: replug recovery must first account for an in-flight transaction.

The read-only dcpext_present sysfs attribute reports mode, retained-buffer
count, completed desktop swaps, front-buffer index and maximum command/wait
duration in microseconds. Counters are diagnostics, not hardware timestamps.
DRM still timestamps completion in software after Wait; this is neither a
vblank interrupt implementation nor zero-copy scanout.
